              "text": "The typical numbers worn by linebackers have changed dramatically in recent years due to sweeping rule updates in both professional and college football. \n\n### Modern NFL Rules (Current)\nCurrently, NFL linebackers are officially allowed to wear **0 to 59** and **90 to 99**.\n\nBecause of these relaxed rules (introduced in 2021 and updated in 2023 to include the number 0), many modern star linebackers now choose single digits or numbers in the 10s, 20s, and 30s:\n* **Roquan Smith** (#0)\n* **Patrick Queen** (#6)\n* **Micah Parsons** (#11)\n* **Nick Bolton** (#32)\n\n---\n\n### The Traditional & Most Common Linebacker Numbers\nDespite the new rules, many players still stick to the classic linebacker number ranges, which are highly recognizable:\n\n#### 1. The 50s (50\u201359) \u2014 *The Ultimate Classic*\nThis is the most iconic range for linebackers. For decades, it was the primary range assigned to the position. Inside and middle linebackers who act as the \"quarterback of the defense\" heavily favor these numbers.\n* **Famous Examples:** Ray Lewis (#52), Lawrence Taylor (#56), Mike Singletary (#50), Brian Urlacher (#54), Luke Kuechly (#59), Fred Warner (#54), and Bobby Wagner (#54).\n\n#### 2. The 90s (90\u201399) \u2014 *The Edge Rushers*\nThis range is highly typical for outside linebackers (often called \"EDGE\" rushers) in 3-4 defensive schemes. These players split their duties between traditional linebacking and rushing the passer like a defensive end. \n* **Famous Examples:** T.J. Watt (#90), Matthew Judon (#9, but formerly #99), and Joey Bosa (#97).\n\n#### 3. The 40s (40\u201349) \u2014 *The Hybrids*\nHistorically, players in the 40s were usually \"coverage\" linebackers, hybrid safety/linebackers, or special teams specialists. \n* **Famous Example:** Patrick Queen wore #48 during his rookie season before switching to #6 when rules changed.\n\n---\n\n### History of the NFL Linebacker Numbering Rules\nIf you are wondering why these numbers are \"typical,\" it is because the NFL strictly regulated them for nearly 50 years to help officials, players, and fans easily identify positions on the field:\n* **Pre-1984:** Linebackers were strictly restricted to the **50\u201359** range.\n* **1984:** Due to the popularity of the 3-4 defense, teams began running out of numbers in the 50s. The NFL opened up the **90\u201399** range to linebackers.\n* **2004:** The NFL allowed linebackers to wear **40\u201349**.\n* **2021:** The league passed a landmark rule change allowing linebackers to wear **1\u201359** and **90\u201399**. This was largely driven by players wanting to keep the single-digit numbers they wore in college.\n* **2023:** The NFL approved the use of **No. 0**, making the final allowed range **0\u201359** and **90\u201399**.\n\n---\n\n### College Football (NCAA) Rules\nIn college football, numbering rules are incredibly relaxed. Linebackers are allowed to wear **any number from 0 to 99**. However, because single digits (0\u20139) and low teens (10\u201319) are highly coveted and carry prestige in college programs, you will very frequently see star college linebackers wearing those smaller numbers.",
